      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2015 American College of Allergy, Asthma & Immunology. Published by Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Diagnosing house dust mite (HDM) allergic rhinitis is difficult. The nasal provocation test (NPT) has been shown to be the most pertinent, but several methods are available. According to guidelines, the NPT requires a skin end-point titration and an objective measurement of nasal patency. Hence, NPT is time consuming and its use is limited."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To evaluate the sensitivity, specificity, and safety of a new, more rapid, and simple alternative NPT (NPT-R) to HDM."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Eighty-eight patients with from rhinitis (49 allergic to HDM and 39 controls with and without atopy) were included. Allergic rhinitis to HDM was confirmed by a \"classic\" NPT based on the Lebel score and rhinomanometry. After a period of 4 weeks, NPT-R was performed and only the clinical score was measured."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "The study population was young (mean ± SD, 27.7 ± 8.5 years old), composed mostly of women (61 vs 27 men), and 24% reported asthma. The sensitivity and specificity of NPT-R were 83.7% and 100%, respectively. The correlation between the NPTs was statistically significant (0.833, P < .0001, n = 88) and the 2 NPTs were completely safe. Performing NPT-R was more rapid (mean ± SD, 22 ± 8 minutes) than the classic NPT (97 ± 20 minutes)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "The NPT-R is safe and easier and faster than the classic NPT. This new method appears to be a very useful tool in the diagnosis of HDM allergic rhinitis when the diagnosis is uncertain or before initiating immunotherapy."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "TRIAL REGISTRATION",
            "content": "ClinicalTrials.gov, identifier NCT01485523."
          }
        ]
      },
